<?xml version="1.0"?>
<Devices>
	<Device type="custom" id="macOsHealth">
		<Name>macOS health</Name>
		<UiDisplayStateId>healthStatus</UiDisplayStateId>

		<ConfigUI>
			<Field id="infoLabel1" type="label" fontColor="green">
				<Label>Measures the health of the Mac that runs this Indigo server: cpu, memory, disk,
  network and the busiest app.
cpu: % of the capacity of all cores. 
Average and max (peak of 15 sec readings) are for the measure interval
  (5 minutes; the average is made of 2 readings 45 seconds apart).
top app: 100% = one full core, as in "top". All processes with the same name count as one app.
The 24h values are kept in memory and start new after a plugin restart.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="scheduleLabel" type="label" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>Measured every 5 minutes. Each measurement is the average of 2 readings 45 seconds apart 
    (cpu, network, busiest apps). 
  The first one is shown ~90 seconds after the device starts.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="selfReport" type="checkbox" alwaysUseInDialogHeightCalc="true" defaultValue="false">
				<Label>Self reporting: count the cpu used by this plugin itself:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="selfReportLabel" type="label" alignWithControl="true" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>Off (default): what the utilities plugin process and the commands it starts to measure (ps, vm_stat, netstat, ..) 
use is taken out of the cpu values (cpuAvg, cpuUser, cpuSystem, cpuMax) and the plugin is not listed as busiest app. 
On: they are counted like any other program.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="printStatesButton" type="button" alwaysUseInDialogHeightCalc="true" tooltip="writes all states of this device, with units and meaning, and the limits to the Indigo log">
				<Label>Print the states and limits of this device to the log:</Label>
				<Title>Print states</Title>
				<CallbackMethod>macHealthPrintStates</CallbackMethod>
			</Field>
			<Field id="printStatesLabel" type="label" alignWithControl="true" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>shows the values of the saved device, save changes first (OK) and reopen to see changed limits</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="netInterfaces" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>Network interfaces to add up:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="netInterfacesLabel" type="label" alignWithControl="true" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>comma separated, e.g. en0,en1. Leave empty for all ethernet / wifi interfaces (en0, en1, ..) 
that are not part of a bridge. Local traffic (lo0) is never counted unless you list it.</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="bridgeInterfaces" type="textfield" defaultValue="bridge0">
				<Label>Bridge (thunderbolt link) to count separately:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="bridgeInterfacesLabel" type="label" alignWithControl="true" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>comma separated, default bridge0 = thunderbolt link between two Macs (screen sharing, file copy). 
 Its traffic goes into the bridgeInKBps / bridgeOutKBps states and not into the network states above. 
 Empty = off. Shown only if the interface exists.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="statesSep" type="separator"/>
			<Field id="statesLabel" type="label" fontColor="green">
				<Label>== What the states of this device mean ==</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelCpu0" type="label" fontColor="orange">
				<Label>
CPU</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelCpu" type="label" fontSize="small">
				<Label>All cpu values are in % of the capacity of ALL cores together: 
  100% = every core busy all the time, 50% = half of the total capacity.

cpuAvg: how busy the cpu was on average during the 2 readings of the measurement (45 seconds each). 
  Idle time is not counted. 
  cpuAvg = cpuUser + cpuSystem.

cpuUser: the part of cpuAvg used by programs (apps, Indigo and its plugins, background tasks).

cpuSystem: the part of cpuAvg used by macOS itself for the programs: 
  disk and network traffic, drivers, memory handling. 
  A high cpuSystem with a low cpuUser often points to heavy disk, network or memory swapping.

cpuMax: the highest cpu reading in the interval. 
 The cpu is also read every 15 seconds in between and the highest of these short readings is kept, 
 so a spike of 30 seconds that the average hides is still visible. Always cpuMax >= cpuAvg.
 The cpu used by this plugin itself is left out of these values unless "Self reporting" above is ticked.

cpuAvg24h / cpuMax24h: the average of all cpuAvg values, and the highest cpuMax, of the last 24 hours 
  (kept in memory, they start new after a plugin restart).

cpuCores: number of cores. load1 / load5 / load15: load average of the last 1, 5, 15 minutes 
  = how many programs wanted to run at the same time. 
  About the number of cores = fully used, much higher = programs have to wait.

Example: cpuAvg 20%, cpuUser 15%, cpuSystem 5%, cpuMax 85%: quiet most of the time, 
  one short burst that used 85% of the capacity.</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelMem0" type="label" fontColor="orange">
				<Label>
MEMORY</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelMem" type="label" fontSize="small">
				<Label>memUsedPct / memUsedGB / memTotalGB: memory used as Activity Monitor counts it (apps + wired + compressed). 
  File cache is NOT counted, it is released when needed.

memAvg24h / memMax24h: average and highest memUsedPct of the last 24 hours.

memPressure: how hard the system works to get free memory: normal, warning, critical. More telling than memUsedPct.

swapUsedMB: memory pushed out to disk. Some swap is normal, a lot and growing means the Mac needs more memory.</Label>
			</Field> 
			<Field id="statesLabelDisk0" type="label" fontColor="orange">
				<Label>
DISK, NETWORK, APPS</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelDisk" type="label" fontSize="small">
				<Label>diskUsedPct / diskFreeGB: startup disk.

netInKBps / netOutKBps: data received / sent, average KB/s over the measure interval 
  (all ethernet + wifi, or the interfaces you list above).

netInMax24h / netOutMax24h: highest interval average of the last 24 hours.

bridgeInKBps / bridgeOutKBps / bridgeInMax24h / bridgeOutMax24h: 
  the same for the thunderbolt bridge (bridge0) between two Macs, e.g. screen sharing. 
  Counted separately, it does not count for the network states and limits above.

topProcName / topProcCpu / topProcList: app with the highest cpu use in the interval, 
  its cpu in % of ONE core (100 = one full core, can be above 100), 
  and the top 3. Same name = one app.

mountCount / tmSnapshotMounts / authdMemMB: mounted volumes, how many of them are Time Machine snapshot volumes, 
  and the memory of the macOS service authd. macOS checks an authorization for EVERY mounted volume twice a minute 
  and authd leaks a little memory each time, so hundreds of snapshot mounts make authd grow to many GB over weeks. 
  Normal: a handful of mounts, 0 .. few snapshot mounts, authd in the tens of MB.

uptimeHours: hours since the Mac was started.</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelStatus0" type="label" fontColor="orange">
				<Label>
STATUS</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="statesLabelStatus" type="label" fontSize="small">
				<Label>healthStatus: starting (waiting for the first measurement, ~90 sec) / ok / warning (a limit below is exceeded). 
This is the state shown in the device list.

warnings: which limits are exceeded, e.g. "cpu | mem". "none" if all is fine.

lastMeasured: time of the last measurement. If it stops moving the plugin is not running.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="limitsSep" type="separator"/>
			<Field id="limitsEnabled" type="checkbox" defaultValue="false">
				<Label>Print a warning to the log when a limit is exceeded:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="limitsLabel" type="label" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>Limits are only checked when the box above is ticked. Leave a field empty to not check that value.
A limit is reported only when it is exceeded at two measurements in a row (a single short peak is ignored). 
  The warning then repeats at every measurement while the value stays beyond the limit; 
  a log line tells when it is back to normal.
  The state "healthStatus" shows "warning" meanwhile, use it for triggers.</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="cpuLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="90">
				<Label>CPU average (cpuAvg, over the measure interval) is above [% of all cores]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="cpuMaxLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>CPU max (cpuMax, highest short reading in the interval) is above [% of all cores]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="topAppLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>busiest app uses more than [% of one core, 100 = 1 core]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="loadLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>load average (5 min) is above [load]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="memLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="90">
				<Label>memory used is above [%]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="pressureLimit" type="menu" defaultValue="">
				<Label>memory pressure reaches:</Label>
				<List>
					<Option value=""         >do not check</Option>
					<Option value="warning"  >warning (or critical)</Option>
					<Option value="critical" >critical</Option>
				</List>
			</Field>
			<Field id="swapLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>swap used is above [MB]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="diskLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="90">
				<Label>startup disk used is above [%]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="diskFreeLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>startup disk free is below [GB]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="netInMbLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>network in (average of the 2 readings) is above [Mb/s, megabit per second]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="netOutMbLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="">
				<Label>network out (average of the 2 readings) is above [Mb/s, megabit per second]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="tmSep" type="separator"/>
			<Field id="tmLabel" type="label" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgrey">
				<Label>TIME MACHINE BACKUP VOLUMES
While Time Machine makes a backup it can mount its old backups as volumes, one per backup, so hundreds
  when there are many backups, and it may give them back only slowly. macOS checks an authorization for every
  mounted volume twice a minute and its service authd leaks a little memory with each check, so authd can grow
  over the weeks. Whether this happens, and how fast, depends on the macOS version and on how many backups exist.
  Choose what the plugin checks:
  - watch authd only: no warning for the number of volumes, only when authd gets too large (limit below).
    That warning tells the command to unmount the backup volumes by hand (it shows each volume).
  - warn above the volume limits: warn when more volumes than the two volume limits below are mounted.
    Set the limits above the number that is normal on this Mac (see the states mountCount and tmSnapshotMounts).
  The plugin itself never unmounts anything, that is only done by hand:
    sudo bash "&lt;plugin folder&gt;/tmUnmount/tm_unmount_snapshots.sh"      (the warning shows the full path)</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="tmMode" type="menu" defaultValue="watch">
				<Label>Time Machine backup volumes:</Label>
				<List>
					<Option value="watch">watch authd only, unmount by hand when it grows (recommended)</Option>
					<Option value="limit">warn when more volumes than the limits below are mounted</Option>
				</List>
			</Field>
			<Field id="mountsLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="60">
				<Label>mounted volumes (mountCount) is above  [only for: warn above the volume limits]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="tmMountsLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="20">
				<Label>Time Machine backup volumes mounted (tmSnapshotMounts) is above 
  [only for: warn above the volume limits]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="authdLimit" type="textfield" defaultValue="500">
				<Label>authd memory (authdMemMB) is above [MB]:</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="expertAdvice" type="checkbox" alwaysUseInDialogHeightCalc="true" defaultValue="true">
				<Label>Expert advice on/off:
  the first warning about authd or the backup volumes also tells how to fix it (the commands):</Label>
			</Field>

			<Field id="mcSep" type="separator"/>
			<Field id="warnMulticastDenied" type="checkbox" alwaysUseInDialogHeightCalc="true" defaultValue="true">
				<Label>Warn when an app is denied Local Network (multicast):</Label>
			</Field>
			<Field id="warnMulticastDeniedLabel" type="label" alignWithControl="true" fontSize="small" fontColor="darkgray">
				<Label>Each measurement does a LIVE multicast send from the Indigo process (state localNetworkMulticast =
ok / blocked). If it is blocked (errno 65) Indigo cannot reach the LAN - multicast and TCP/UDP to local devices
fail while the internet still works (e.g. a Hue bridge goes unreachable). The warning fires once when it flips to
blocked (not every measurement). Fix: System Settings > Privacy &amp; Security > Local Network, enable Indigo, then
reboot.
It also reads the stored consent store (com.apple.networkextension.plist) for info only - states multicastDeniedCount
/ multicastDeniedApps list apps with DenyMulticast=true - but that stored policy can lag the live state, so the
warning uses the live test, not the policy.</Label>
			</Field>
		</ConfigUI>

